WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.960
In how many ways can we arrange six people in a circle?

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.720
Yes, we have seen problems in which we have arranged people in a row.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.860
But this time we have to arrange them in a circular fashion.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.020
Six people will be seated like this.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.280
In how many ways can we arrange six people around the circle?

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.940
If we had to arrange them in a row,

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.280
we know that it can be done in six factorial ways.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.020
Will it be the same if they have to be seated in a circular fashion?

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.200
Most of us know the answer here, it will not be the same.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.940
It's six minus one factorial.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.120
These many ways in which we can arrange six people in a circular fashion.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.000
But why? Why do we subtract a one?

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.600
And this is exactly what most don't know.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.640
And most of us won't even try to understand it.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.620
Because the simple formula.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.040
"N" people around a circular table,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.880
can be arranged in "N minus 1" factorial ways.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.720
Let's understand why.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.600
First we need to know the number of ways

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.620
in which we can arrange six people in a row.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.300
We know its six factorial. But do you remember how we arrived at it?

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.080
We had made six slots for the six people.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.880
And the first question we asked ourselves was,

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.960
in how many different ways can the first person be seated?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.300
He can be seated in any one of these six slots.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.280
And each slot is different from the other.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.300
It means the first person can be seated in six ways.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.140
So let's say the first person is seated.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.680
In how many ways can the second person be seated now?

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.860
As one slot is gone, just five remain.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.600
The second person can be seated in five different ways.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.900
On any one of the five remaining slots.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.320
This goes on and we get the answer as six factorial.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.500
That's how we had six factorial.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.340
Now that we know this, let's move on to the circular arrangement case.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.820
In how many ways can we arrange six people in a circular fashion?

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.020
Let's draw a circle.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.460
Assume it's a circular table with chairs around it.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.040
Six people can be seated like this.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.700
Now let's follow the same approach to get to the answer.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.920
In how many different ways can the first person be seated here?

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.500
It's simple, don't worry!

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.700
As there are six chairs around the table,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.080
the first person can be seated in six different ways.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.360
Correct?

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.960
No, it's not correct.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.260
The first person that comes to the table

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.320
cannot be seated in six different ways.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:49.360
To understand the number of ways in which the first person can be seated.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.860
Let's look at a circular table with six chairs around it.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.540
Now assume you're the first person.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.740
And let's say you sit on this chair.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.720
What do you see?

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.740
Try to describe the positions of the chair you see.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:08.980
You see three chairs in front, one on your left. And one on your right.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:14.180
Now assume you have decided to sit here on this chair. What do you see?

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.960
You see three chairs in front, one on your left, and one on your right.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.120
It's the same wheel. It means you sitting on this chair,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.860
is not any different from you sitting on the previous chair.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.260
Say you had decided to sit on the third chair. The view is still the same.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.420
Three in front, one on your left, and one on your right.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:37.640
Come on,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.220
What does this tell us?

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.120
It tells us that there is just one way in which

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.180
the first person can be seated on any of the six chairs.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.040
Let's go back to the whiteboard now.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.480
So we saw that the first person can be seated in just one way.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.560
Now we get to the interesting question.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.140
Assuming that the first person is seated on one of the chairs,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.040
in how many different ways can the second person be seated?

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.800
Try answering this question.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.820
Okay, let's see.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.600
Assume that the first person is seated here.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.320
Five chairs are left for the second person.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.880
If the second person sits here think of his view.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.280
He has three chairs in front one on the right, and one on the left.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.880
But wait, that's not enough.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.320
The chair on the left has the first person seated.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:37.620
So it's three in the front, one on the right, and one with a person on the left.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.620
Let's say the second guy is seated here.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.700
This time we have three chairs in front,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.900
one on the left and one with the person on the right.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.220
After the first person is seated,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.340
this chair is different from this one for the second person.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.220
Similarly, the other three are also totally different from the other chairs.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.400
The ways become different second time onwards.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.900
Because of this reference point.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.500
The second person can be seated in five different ways.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:11.760
And similarly the third person can be seated in four different ways,

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.860
assuming the first two are already seated.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.380
And this goes on till everybody is seated.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.880
The answer will be '1 multiplied by 5 factorial'.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.840
That equals 5 Factorial.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.880
This was the logic behind subtracting '1 from N'.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.640
The first person can be seated in just one way,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.360
If It is a row, then each slot or chair is different

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.720
from the others for the first person.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.480
But here each chair or position is the same for the first person.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.260
So remember, "N" people can be seated in a circular fashion

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.600
in "N minus 1" factorial ways.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.960
Memorising this is not a good idea.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.240
Understanding the concept is better.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.620
And you will understand why, when you see the next video.

